WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.070
Galaktoboureko and baklava are traditional
Greek desserts.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.870
Although several European and Middle Eastern
countries have versions of baklava, most

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:13.130
consist of phyllo pastry filled with nuts
drenched in honey or syrup.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:18.080
Galaktoboureko is phyllo filled with custard
and coated with sweet syrup.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:24.230
This variety of baklava called Berma is made
not with phyllo dough like classic baklava,

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:27.200
but with kataifi shredded wheat dough.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:33.860
Berma like classic phyllo baklava contains
whole pistachios, never pistachio powder.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:38.570
This Berma chef pours granulated sugar over
the pistachio kernels.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.180
Next, he sprinkles rosewater.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.760
Although this ingredient is optional.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:50.120
Then syrup made with sugar, water, cinnamon
and nutmeg as well as lemon to prevent the

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.950
sugar from later crystallizing.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.370
He mixes by hand to thoroughly coat the
kernels.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:02.190
The kataifi is made of all purpose flour,
milk and water.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:10.560
The chef lays out a section of dough, then
takes a handful of pistachio filling and

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:15.930
places it on the dough, forming a line
running down the middle for a Burma loaf this

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.390
size. The filling is about an inch wide and
high.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:27.210
He folds the end closed and rolls the dough
at an angle down the length of the Burma,

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.070
tightly encasing the filling as he goes.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.140
At the end, he folds the opposite end
closed.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.860
It takes a Burma chef months to perfect this
technique.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:46.870
He places it on a tray to air dry for 24
hours to ensure the dough maintains its old

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:53.850
shape. Unlike classic baklava, which is
baked, Burma is fried, and the risk of

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.770
burning the pistachios makes this the most
technically challenging type of baklava to

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:05.610
make. The chef fries the loaves for three
minutes in oil heated to around 350°F,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.500
turning them once per minute.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.860
He lets the oil drain off for half an hour.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:18.030
Then he pours warm, caramelized sugar over
the loaves and lets them soak for 20 minutes.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:23.970
The sugar to water ratio and the boiling
time of the caramelized sugar have to be just

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:29.250
right. Otherwise it will become either too
hard or too gooey when it cools.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.790
After letting the excess drain off the
loaves rest for about three hours.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:44.120
Then the chef rolls the loaf to flatten out
the bumps and slices it into equal pieces.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.950
But if there's a protracted battle waging
between your sweet tooth and your willpower.

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:59.290
You've got plenty of time to work your way
through a box of Burma because it stays fresh

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:00.490
for two weeks.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.780
To make galaktoboureko.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:11.400
The chef begins with two ingredients milk
and semolina.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:18.060
After bringing the milk to a boil, he
gradually adds the semolina while whisking

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.620
and lowering the temperature.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.800
Once the mix thickens, he sets it aside to
cool.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:32.160
In a separate bowl, the chef whisks eggs,
adds vanilla, then lemon or orange rind.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:36.270
Other options are orange blossom or
rosewater extract.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.850
He transfers the ingredients to an electric
mixer, adds butter and sugar.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.610
Then he adds the semolina and milk mixture.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:49.320
This mixture must be cool so that the eggs
don't cook.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:56.040
Once everything is thoroughly blended, the
Galaktoboureko custard is ready.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:02.300
The chef now turns his attention to the
phyllo dough.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:07.940
He stacks 24 parchment, thin sheets of phyllo
and slices them into squares.

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:12.260
He places one scoop full of custard in the
center of each square.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:18.350
The traditional Greek way is to roll the
squares or bake a large galaktoboureko in a

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.960
pan and then cut it into squares.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:25.940
However, this chef folds over each square to
make Lebanese style triangles.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:32.390
He douses the top with clarified butter
butter that's been boiled to separate out the

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:34.670
solids and leave just the oil.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:37.910
Then he bakes the pastries for 45 minutes at
325°F.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:44.810
The golden baked top shines thanks to the
clarified butter.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:48.590
The chef cooks the same syrup he made for
the baklava.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:54.950
Sugar water, cinnamon, nutmeg and lemon and
generously drizzles the mixture all over the

00:04:54.950 --> 00:05:00.630
galaktoboureko as he sprinkles on cinnamon
for traditional flavor and some crushed

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:02.610
pistachios for decoration.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.920
The annual apple harvest is just two months
long.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:11.740
To keep apples fresh in storage for several
months, growers lower the store room oxygen

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:14.080
level to put the apples into hibernation.

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:20.370
Then they use machines called CO2 scrubbers
to absorb and remove the carbon dioxide gas

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:21.700
The apples give off.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:26.560
This CO2 scrubber pulls air from the
airtight apple storage room through carbon

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.830
pellets, which absorb the CO2 gas molecules.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:34.270
The machine then blows the CO2 free air back
into the room.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.970
Workers build, the machines frame out of
tubular steel.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:43.570
The frame supports all the components,
including two sealed vessels filled with

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:44.710
carbon pellets.

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:50.890
The first vessel starts absorbing CO2, a
process called scrubbing until its pellets

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:51.940
are saturated.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.580
Then the second vessel takes over the
scrubbing.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:00.580
The first vessel automatically kicks into a
regeneration cycle, which blows fresh air

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:06.230
through the pellets to remove the CO2
molecules and exhaust them out the building.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:12.080
When the second vessel's pellets max out,
vessel one resumes scrubbing while vessel two

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:18.380
regenerates. To construct each vessel,
workers weld a steel sheet into a circular

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:22.010
shape, then weld on a base and a top ring.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:26.120
The ring has holes along its perimeter for
bolting on a lid.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:31.190
They use a ring template to make
corresponding holes in the lid, making the

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.800
exact center of each hole with a punch tool.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:41.030
Then a worker mounts the lid on a fixture,
and following those marks uses an automated

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:43.250
steel punch to perforate the steel.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.130
The vessels go to the paint booth for a coat
of powder paint.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:56.040
Then into an oven to bake the paint.

00:06:56.070 --> 00:07:00.930
When they come out, the vessels are ready to
be filled with carbon pellets and seal.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:08.330
Once filled to the top workers lay in a
steel baffle.

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:14.240
Its holes evenly distribute air passing
through the vessel.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:19.250
A worker then places a foam rubber gasket
around the perimeter.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:23.650
And bolts on the lid tightly.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:29.440
This seals the vessel and prevents the air
being blown through it from leaking out.

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:34.930
The sealed lid also prevents oxygen from
getting into the machine and from there into

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:36.370
the apple storage room.

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:42.340
Next, workers install the PVC piping that
feeds air through the vessels, along with two

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:48.280
blowers. One blower pulls air from the apple
storage room into the machine for scrubbing.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.830
The other sends it back to the room after
scrubbing.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.260
The airflow through the pipes is controlled
by valves which are wired to the machine's

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:00.400
electrical panel.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:07.870
That electrical panel consisting of relays,
pumps, gas analyzers and a touch screen is

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:13.090
built in house in accordance with the
required electrical standards of the

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:14.290
customer's country.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:20.500
The panel is wired to a computer which
controls the entire operation.

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:28.680
Then a worker installs an electrical box
into the machine's frame.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.900
And he installs the electrical panel inside
the box.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:49.110
Next, the worker connects the wires to the
valves, installs the touchscreen and hooks it

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:50.340
up to the computer.

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:57.450
The touchscreen lets the machine's operator
program oxygen and CO2 target levels specific

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.670
to the type of apple stored in the room.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:05.640
This is critical as different varieties of
apples give off more CO2 than others.

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:08.880
How does it all work?

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:12.990
The grower stacks bins of apples in an
airtight storeroom.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:20.250
A nitrogen generator feeds in nitrogen to
reduce the room's oxygen level to 1.8%.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:26.190
When that happens, the nitrogen feed
automatically stops and the continuous CO2

00:09:26.220 --> 00:09:28.380
scrubbing automatically begins.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.620
Many growers have in-house labs which
conduct quality control tests on samples

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:37.980
taken from each lot of scrubbed apples
coming out of storage.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:44.710
No apples would pass these tests after the
two month harvest season if not for the CO2

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:51.070
scrubbers. Co2 scrubbers lengthen an apples
freshness by about eight months, making it

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:53.500
possible to bite into crispy apples year
round.

